{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"beaming-snively-urial","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Problem\n`aggregate.py`'s `_severity_rank`/`_SEVERITY_RANK` (an ordinal string->int map over the\n5-level severity vocabulary) picks the representative finding in a duplicate cluster and\nsorts the final merged list, but the numeric `priority` (`validity x impact`) computed by\n`pass3_decide` is dropped before findings reach `aggregate.py`: `shim.py`'s\n`_to_common_finding` only carries `severity` forward, not `priority`. This is the exact bug\nmotivating the epic: a high-impact/low-validity finding can rank above a genuinely\nhigh-priority one because ranking is impact-only.\n\n## Approach\n1. Thread `priority` (and `decision`) through `code_review/shim.py`'s `_to_common_finding` so\n   the common-finding shape `aggregate.py` consumes carries the numeric value already computed\n   by `pass3_decide` (or set on DET-tier findings by the sibling priority-backfill ticket).\n2. Replace `aggregate.py`'s `_severity_rank`/`_SEVERITY_RANK` with a direct read of\n   `finding[\"priority\"]` at both call sites: representative selection (`aggregate_findings`)\n   and the final sort. Delete `_severity_rank`/`_SEVERITY_RANK`.\n3. Update `spec_scan.py`'s import/use of `_severity_rank` to sort by `priority` directly.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `shim.py::_to_common_finding` includes `priority` in its output dict for every finding.\n- [ ] `aggregate.py::aggregate_findings` selects the cluster representative by\n  `finding[\"priority\"]` (ties broken as today by detail length); `_severity_rank` and\n  `_SEVERITY_RANK` are deleted.\n- [ ] `spec_scan.py` sorts by `priority` instead of the deleted `_severity_rank` import.\n- [ ] `pytest tests/unit/test_llm_aggregate.py` (locate via\n  `pytest --collect-only -q -k aggregate`) passes, with a strengthened test asserting a\n  higher-impact/lower-validity finding no longer wins representative selection over a\n  lower-impact/higher-validity one with higher priority.\n- [ ] `grep -rn \"_severity_rank\\|_SEVERITY_RANK\" src/rebar/llm/` returns no matches.\n\n## Testing\nTier: in-process unit test against the real `aggregate_findings`/representative-selection\nfunction, fed production-shaped finding dicts (contract test: real producer shape -> real\nconsumer). Oracle: which finding is selected as representative and the sort order of the\nmerged list, asserted by finding identity. Contrast case: a high-impact/low-validity finding\n(low priority) paired with a moderate-impact/high-validity one (higher priority) -- assert\nthe second wins.","id":"ce15-28c5-80e0-49c2","parent_id":"c4c8-317a-85ca-4b08","priority":2,"repos":["rebar"],"tags":[],"ticket_type":"task","title":"Rank aggregation by priority instead of severity"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788112384643776064,"uuid":"de8dd7f2-a23b-4550-bceb-72c1046cfb89"}